## LEVEL I STATUS

**STILL OPEN.**

## 1. Starting endpoint and implication chain

The sufficient Target B closure is
\[
G+E\ge {\sqrt3\over4}B+{\sqrt3\over2}(t+\chi-1).
\]
For a pure rhombic disk, a global minimizer has the strict reverse inequality
`G+E < (sqrt(3)/4) B`. The canonical phase-circle cut PC deletes a set `F` of
rhombi with `|F| <= (2+sqrt(3)) E` and makes the retained train crossing graph
phase coherent on each component.

The intended chain was

```text
edge-grain separator theorem
 -> source phase-grain area concentration at a sharper interface cost
 -> global multi-grain shell/diameter contradiction
 -> Target B and Level I closure.
```

The first arrow is proved below. The second is not: `G+E<c_0B` permits an
interface budget of order `D`, and the present relative-isoperimetric lower bound
on that budget is too small to contradict it.

## 2. Two-face interface theorem

**Classification:** proved candidate awaiting independent audit; noncanonical.

Let `U` be a finite, edge-to-edge union of unit rhombi, let `K=conv(U)`, and
delete any set `F` of rhombus faces. Partition the retained faces into **edge
grains**: two faces are in the same grain when a chain of retained faces joins
them across full shared edges. Let `S` be any union of edge grains and let `A`
be its area. Put
\[
Q_U={\cal H}^1(\partial U\cap\operatorname{int}K),
\qquad f=|F|,
\qquad V=|K|.
\]
Then there is a union `W` of `S` with either all or none of the deleted faces,
with `A'=|W|` satisfying `A<=A'<=A+f`, such that
\[
\boxed{\ {cal H}^1(\partial W\cap\operatorname{int}K)
       \le Q_U+2f.\ }
\tag{1}
\]
Consequently the exact finite inequality
\[
\boxed{\ \sqrt{\pi A}+\sqrt{\pi(V-A)}
 \le {\operatorname{per}(K)\over2}+Q_U+2f+\sqrt{\pi f}.\ }
\tag{2}
\]
holds. No connectivity of `W` or its complement is assumed. The statement does
not require `F` to come from PC, so it applies in particular to a PC cut.

### Proof

Color each retained edge grain red if it belongs to `S`, and blue otherwise.
No edge joins a red retained face directly to a blue retained face, by the
definition of edge grain. Let `r` and `b` be the numbers of unit edges from
deleted faces to red and blue retained faces, respectively. Each deleted
rhombus has four sides, so `r+b<=4f`; edges between two deleted faces and
edges on the outer boundary contribute to neither count.

Color every deleted face red. The resulting red-blue interior interface has
length exactly `b`. Color every deleted face blue instead; the interface has
length exactly `r`. Choose the smaller option. Its interface is at most
`(r+b)/2<=2f`. The boundary of the chosen red union `W` inside `K` consists
of this interface plus portions of the original boundary of `U` inside `K`.
The latter have total length at most `Q_U`, proving (1). Every deleted unit
rhombus has area at most one, hence `A<=A'<=A+f`.

For any finite-perimeter `W subset K`, the perimeter identity and the planar
isoperimetric inequality give
\[
\sqrt{\pi A'}+\sqrt{\pi(V-A')}
 \le {\operatorname{per}(K)\over2}
       +{\cal H}^1(\partial W\cap\operatorname{int}K).
\]
If `A'=A`, this and (1) already imply (2). Otherwise
`0<=A'-A<=f`, so `sqrt(A)<=sqrt(A')` and
`sqrt(V-A)<=sqrt(V-A')+sqrt(f)`. Adding these inequalities proves (2).
All perimeter identities are unaffected by vertex-only contacts, which have
zero one-dimensional measure.  QED.

### Near-disk consequence

Assume a sequence of such complexes has diameter `D->infinity`,
`V=pi D^2/4+O(D)`, `per(K)=pi D+O(1)`, `f=O(D)`, and retained area
`V-O(D)`. If its largest edge grain has area at most `(1-eta)V` for fixed
`0<eta<=1/2`, then
\[
\boxed{\ {Q_U+2f\over D}\ge
 {\pi\over2}\left(\sqrt{\eta/4}+\sqrt{1-\eta/4}-1\right)-o(1).\ }
\tag{3}
\]
Indeed, select a union of edge grains with area between `eta V/4` and `V/2`:
take the complement of a largest grain if it has area at least `V/2`; take
that grain if its area lies between `eta V/4` and `V/2`; otherwise add grains
greedily until reaching `eta V/4`. The retained-area hypothesis makes each
choice possible for large `D`. Substitute this union into (2). Since
`sqrt(f)=O(sqrt D)=o(D)`, divide by `D` and use that
`sqrt(x)+sqrt(1-x)-1` increases on `[0,1/2]`.

Each edge grain belongs to one connected component of the retained train
crossing graph: rhombi sharing an edge share a train, so their crossing edges
share a train-graph vertex. Thus if `(Q_U+2f)/D->0`, (3) forces one actual
retained train component to contain `V-o(D^2)` area. Under the canonical PC
cut, `E=o(D)` and `Q_U=o(D)` suffice for this conclusion because
`f<=(2+sqrt(3))E=o(D)` and the pure-disk shell window gives `G=O(D)`.

**Canonical-scope audit.** The displayed small-`Q_U` premise is impossible
for a sufficiently large pure-rhombic-disk global-minimizer survivor. In the
canonical Target B Section 39, `B=2T` and
\[
 \liminf B/D\ge5.0790016366\ldots.
\]
The perimeter of boundary edges of `U` lying on `partial K` is at most
`P=per(K)<=pi D`; all other unit boundary edges contribute to `Q_U`.
Consequently
\[
 \boxed{\liminf Q_U/D\ge5.0790016366\ldots-\pi
       =1.9374089830\ldots.}
\tag{4}
\]
Thus the `Q_U=o(D)` corollary in the unreviewed PR #564 candidate, and the
matching conditional corollary above, cannot be invoked on the canonical
pure-disk survivor. This does not invalidate the finite relative-isoperimetric
theorem. It identifies a missing source-level bridge: the unavoidable inward
boundary length must be paid or canceled, not assumed sublinear.

### Sharpness of the interface coefficient

Take the plus-shaped union of five unit squares: a central square and its
four edge neighbors. Delete the center, and let `S` consist of the north and
south arms. The four arms are four distinct edge grains. Assigning the center
to either color leaves exactly two red-blue unit edges. Hence the coefficient
`2f` in (1) cannot be reduced for arbitrary deleted-face sets, even for a
simply connected strict unit-rhombus disk. This is a face-interface example;
it is not claimed to be a PC cut or a global minimizer.

## 3. Vertex-contact regression and scope

Euclidean connected components of the **closed** retained-face union need not
be phase grains. In a `2 x 2` unit-square grid, delete the northwest and
southeast squares. The remaining northeast and southwest squares meet at the
central vertex, so their closed union is Euclidean connected. Their crossing
graph has two disjoint edges, one for each square, and thus two train
components. This exact finite example invalidates the general inference
`Euclidean connected retained-face component => one train component`.

PR #564's relative-isoperimetric inequality for Euclidean components is not
disproved by this example; its transfer from a largest Euclidean component to
a largest **phase** grain needs the edge-grain correction. The present theorem
supplies that correction and sharpens its interface coefficient at the same
time. No assertion is made that the diagonal deletion in this example is a
canonical PC cut; the new theorem holds for every cut, including PC cuts.

This proof concerns only the physical rhombic complex. It does not identify
the complex with all source points, control contact-isolated source points,
handle exceptional faces or multiple complexes, or give a strict sub-`D`
source competitor.

### A shallow two-rhombus pocket cannot pay `Q_U` locally

There is no positive universal constant `a` such that every triangle-free
strict unit-rhombus disk satisfies `G+E>=a Q_U`. For `epsilon>0` small, set
\[
 v=(1,0),\quad
 u=(\cos(\pi/3+\epsilon),\sin(\pi/3+\epsilon)),\quad
 w=-(\cos(\pi/3+2\epsilon),\sin(\pi/3+2\epsilon)).
\]
Join the unit rhombi with vertex lists `(0,v,v+u,u)` and
`(0,v,v+w,w)` along their common edge `[0,v]`. Their acute angles are
`pi/3+epsilon` and `pi/3+2epsilon`. The six vertices are unit-separated
for all sufficiently small positive `epsilon`: at `epsilon=0`, the only
non-edge unit pairs are the two short diagonals `(v,u)` and `(0,v+w)`;
their lengths become
`sqrt(2-2cos(pi/3+epsilon))>1` and
`sqrt(2-2cos(pi/3+2epsilon))>1`. All other non-edge distances are strictly
greater than one at zero and remain so by continuity. For completeness, the
eight non-edge pairs and their limiting distances as `epsilon -> 0+` are

| Pair | Limiting distance |
| --- | ---: |
| `(0,v+u)`, `(v,w)` | `sqrt(3)` |
| `(0,v+w)`, `(v,u)` | `1`, strictly increasing above `1` for `epsilon>0` |
| `(u,w)`, `(v+u,v+w)` | `2` |
| `(u,v+w)` | `sqrt(3)` |
| `(v+u,w)` | `sqrt(7)` |

The seven rhombus-edge pairs remain exactly unit length. Thus the unit-contact
graph is two 4-cycles sharing one edge and has no unit triangle.

The outer boundary runs through `0,u,v+u,v,v+w,w`. At `v` it makes a
concave turn of `epsilon`, while the other turn created at `0` is convex.
The convex hull replaces the two unit edges incident to `v` by their chord.
Hence `Q_U=2` and the pocket area is
\[
 G=\tfrac12|\det(u,w)|=\tfrac12\sin\epsilon.
\]
The angular surplus is
\[
 E=\sin(\pi/3+\epsilon)+\sin(\pi/3+2\epsilon)-\sqrt3
   =O(\epsilon).
\]
Consequently `(G+E)/Q_U ->0`. This is an exact feasible finite regression,
not a global minimizer or a near-disk sequence. It kills a **local** pocket
payment rule; a source-specific global inequality may still use active
diameter constraints or the full boundary order.

## 4. Frontier effect and remaining lemma

The finite multi-grain concentration threshold improves from `Q_U+4|F|` in
the unreviewed PR #564 candidate to `Q_U+2|F|` at the `D` scale, with the
grain defined so that concentration transfers to a retained train component.
Equation (4) shows that this refinement alone does not narrow the canonical
pure-disk survivor: `Q_U` is necessarily linear and already exceeds the
small-interface premise. In fact the largest possible leading excess on the
left of the relative-isoperimetric inequality, after subtracting `P/2`, is
`(pi/2)(sqrt(2)-1)D=0.650645...D`, attained at a half-area split. The
canonical lower bound `Q_U>=1.937408...D-o(D)` is almost three times that
maximum. Thus this relative-isoperimetric inequality is numerically vacuous
for **every** area split in the canonical pure-disk regime, regardless of
whether `4|F|` or `2|F|` is used. The canonical Target B endpoint and Level I
status do not change.

The exact next multi-grain proposition is: for every fixed `eta>0`, exclude a
sequence of triangle-free jammed pure-rhombic-disk global minimizers with
`G+E<c_0B` and every retained train component occupying at most
`(1-eta)R` rhombi. Equation (3) shows that such a sequence must have
`Q_U+2|F|>=c(eta)D-o(D)`, but (4) makes that bound automatic with a larger
constant. A closing proof must couple the **unavoidable** inward boundary
length to `G+E>=c_0B` or a same-cardinality strict sub-`D` competitor;
the two-rhombus regression shows that this cannot be a universal local
payment by `G+E` per inward boundary edge.

